WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:01.735
<u>-Guys, thank you for that.
-Congrats.</u>

00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:04.537
"Almost Famous" on Broadway.
-It's wild.

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:06.572
-Let's talk about this.
This is unbelievable.

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:08.207
I mean, you've directed some of

00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:10.643
the most beloved movies
ever made --

00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:13.679
"Almost Famous,"
"Jerry Maguire,"

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:14.948
"Fast Times at Ridgemont High."

00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:17.583
You've written for
"Rolling Stone."

00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:20.620
I mean, how does it go
from creating

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:25.024
books, articles, movies
to Broadway?

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:27.927
-And getting to direct you
along the way in the movie.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:29.695
-No big deal. Yeah.

00:00:29.695 --> 00:00:33.366
My first big movie role
ever was in "Almost Famous."

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:35.301
No big deal.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:40.407
-Yeah.

00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:44.844
-But is it tough?
Who did you meet with?

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:46.912
You must have studied.
I know you.

00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:48.981
-Yeah.
-You must have jumped into this.

00:00:48.981 --> 00:00:50.349
-I did.

00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:53.018
My friend Lia Vollack
is a huge music fan,

00:00:53.018 --> 00:00:57.290
and she kind of turned into
a Broadway producer of renown.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.393
And she said to me, "You know,
I can make this fun for you."

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:03.629
And "Almost Famous"
is the one thing, I think,

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:08.134
I've been involved with that
had the most recurring fan talk,

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:09.435
and people would always
want to talk about

00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:11.904
the feeling
that the movie gave them.

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:13.773
And I thought, "You know,
if we can get that feeling

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:16.442
on a live stage in a theater,
that's almost like

00:01:16.442 --> 00:01:19.045
the 1973 world that
the movie is based on."

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:20.179
-Yeah.

00:01:20.179 --> 00:01:22.749
-So just to kind of
get that kind of --

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:26.118
You're immersed in 1973,
and that music feeling

00:01:26.118 --> 00:01:29.088
with this cast
has been totally exciting.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:31.490
-Yeah. You get to -- Obviously,

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.960
some of the songs that we know
from the movie are in this.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.762
But there's also some original
great songs in here, as well,

00:01:36.762 --> 00:01:38.698
that you had
your hand in writing.

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:41.934
How is Cameron Crowe
writing a real song?

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:44.236
-Well, you know, I don't have
the body of work that

00:01:44.236 --> 00:01:45.871
"Weird Al" Yankovic has

00:01:45.871 --> 00:01:50.042
or you with "Almost Too Early
for Christmas" and stuff.

00:01:50.042 --> 00:01:52.345
-We're currently in litigation.
We'll figure that out.

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:54.180
-I'm rooting for it.
-Thanks. Me too.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.049
-Yeah. Tom Kitt,
the wonderful composer,

00:01:57.049 --> 00:01:58.784
was involved in this
from the very beginning.

00:01:58.784 --> 00:02:00.719
And we kind of
wrote songs together,

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:04.357
and he showed me how you can
actually jump over into

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:08.894
writing story songs
that the characters can sing.

00:02:08.894 --> 00:02:11.864
And in musical theater,
that's the close-up you get.

00:02:11.864 --> 00:02:12.999
Because they always say,
"Well, you don't get

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:14.867
a close-up in theater,"

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:16.769
but you get those songs,
which give you

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:18.638
the deep feelings of
the characters.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:21.474
And Tom kind of, you know,
was involved in teaching me

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:23.276
how to do that,
and it's been a blast.

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:26.979
-When you went on tour,
no matter who it was,

00:02:26.979 --> 00:02:28.681
you were a kid with
"Rolling Stone."

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:29.882
You were with Led Zeppelin.

00:02:29.882 --> 00:02:31.884
You were with
The Allman Brothers.

00:02:31.884 --> 00:02:35.922
-You never go like,
"Oh, maybe I'll be a rock star.

00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:37.690
Maybe I'll start doing --

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.626
Maybe I'll start writing songs
or performing." No?

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:45.064
-I never could really play and I
never was in a band, really.

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:47.366
But there was one moment
about kind of --

00:02:47.366 --> 00:02:49.502
I think it was 16 or 17,
and I was writing about

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:51.871
The Allman Brothers Band
and on tour with them,

00:02:51.871 --> 00:02:53.072
which is a lot of what

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:55.407
the "Almost famous" story
is based on.

00:02:55.407 --> 00:02:58.444
And we were in this hotel room
in the Continental Hyatt House.

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:00.346
And The Allman Brothers Band
is like a family,

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:03.682
and they've all got guitars and
they're playing this blues song.

00:03:03.682 --> 00:03:05.284
And Gregg Allman
is right in front of me,

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:08.687
and he's playing this blues
song, "Come On in My Kitchen."

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:11.457
And I see this great guitar next
to me that nobody's playing.

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:12.658
-Yeah.

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:14.927
-So I thought, "You know,
this is how it works."

00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:18.998
So, I pick up this guitar,
not knowing how to play at all,

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:20.532
and just start strumming.

00:03:20.532 --> 00:03:22.235
-Yeah.
-And feelin' it.

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:23.369
-Oh, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:25.137
And Gregg Allman
looks over at me

00:03:25.137 --> 00:03:27.707
and then he kind of looks
over -- just over my shoulder

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.008
and gives a nod,
and I'm thinking,

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:31.511
"He digs what I'm doing."

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:33.779
And then, a moment later,
these hands appear

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:37.383
and kind of lift the guitar
out of my hands.

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:41.320
And it was like,
"Your moment in heaven is over."

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:42.622
[ Sad tuba plays ]
You know.

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:44.790
Love the band, like the band,
interview the band.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.658
-Yeah.
-Don't jam with the band.

00:03:46.658 --> 00:03:47.960
-They don't want you
to jam with them.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:48.961
-No.

00:03:48.961 --> 00:03:51.063
-No. When we shot this,

00:03:51.063 --> 00:03:54.667
it was the most fun
experience of my life.

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.537
I had so much fun,
and you loved everybody.

00:03:57.537 --> 00:03:59.172
Everyone loved you.

00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:01.874
It was just magic
when we were doing it.

00:04:01.874 --> 00:04:07.046
And I remember, about halfway
through, maybe towards the end,

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:08.948
there was no real title
for the film.

00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:10.316
-Yeah.
-Do you remember this?

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:11.717
-I do.

00:04:11.717 --> 00:04:14.854
-Or you had an idea for it --
to call it "Untitled"? No.

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:16.956
-I thought we could
call it, like,

00:04:16.956 --> 00:04:19.158
after a Led Zeppelin album,
you know?

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.727
Where it has no title,
it has symbols.

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:26.465
So, the idea was we'd have
this movie that was untitled,

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:29.202
but you could ask for it
by symbols.

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:32.738
And the DreamWorks
department marketing

00:04:32.738 --> 00:04:35.941
really just did not groove
with me on that.

00:04:35.941 --> 00:04:37.276
-So, yeah,
you have this great movie.

00:04:37.276 --> 00:04:38.677
You have no title for it?

00:04:38.677 --> 00:04:40.479
-Yeah, they would say like,
"You know, nobody's going to

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:42.381
come up to the box office
and say,

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:46.185
'I want two tickets for the
symbols movie.'"

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:47.920
Or the untitled movie.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:51.356
-So, we -- As you remember,
we had such a good time

00:04:51.356 --> 00:04:53.726
making the movie,
but we were so addicted to

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:55.494
the idea of no title
that one day,

00:04:55.494 --> 00:04:56.695
we knew we had to
come up with something.

00:04:56.695 --> 00:04:59.699
So, there was
a suggestion box on the set,

00:04:59.699 --> 00:05:01.300
and people would stuff
all these names in there

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:02.935
for what the title could be.

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:06.139
And you, my friend, came up with
a very significant list,

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:08.941
which I kept.

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:10.009
-Are you serious?

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:12.345
-And I would like
to share this with you

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.046
20-some years later.

00:05:14.046 --> 00:05:16.649
Here are your ideas
for the title

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:18.150
of what would become
"Almost Famous."

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:19.785
-This is actually --
This is really my --

00:05:19.785 --> 00:05:21.120
This is how bad
my handwriting is.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.321
You kept this?

00:05:22.321 --> 00:05:24.190
-Yes.
-I'm freaking out. Cameron...

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.391
-No, it's good.
-But this is wild.

00:05:26.391 --> 00:05:27.893
-Yeah.
-This is real.

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:30.096
That's legit.
-Yeah.

00:05:30.096 --> 00:05:32.331
"Vinyl Days"
was my favorite, Jimmy.

00:05:32.331 --> 00:05:34.166
I thought that was pretty great.

00:05:34.166 --> 00:05:38.504
-"Vinyl Days." Not bad.
"I'm with the Band."

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:40.072
[ Light laugher ]

00:05:40.072 --> 00:05:42.541
It's getting chuckles.

00:05:42.541 --> 00:05:44.110
"Pages."

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:45.377
-"Pages." Interesting.
-It's deep.

00:05:45.377 --> 00:05:46.779
-Yeah.

00:05:46.779 --> 00:05:48.414
-'Cause I thought "pages,"
like you're writing pages for

00:05:48.414 --> 00:05:50.983
a magazine, but, also,
it's pages of your life.

00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:54.186
-Exactly. I like --
I'm with you.

00:05:54.186 --> 00:05:55.554
I'm with you on this.

00:05:55.554 --> 00:05:57.456
"I'm on the List"
is kind of interesting.

00:05:57.456 --> 00:06:00.126
-"I'm on the List."
[ Laughs ]

00:06:00.126 --> 00:06:02.694
"The 33 1/3 Incher"?

00:06:02.694 --> 00:06:04.196
-Yeah.
-It's about a record.

00:06:04.196 --> 00:06:05.264
-It's about up there
with the symbols.

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:08.333
-Yeah, yeah, symbols. Yep.

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:10.969
What -- Then I started
getting just silly.

00:06:10.969 --> 00:06:12.638
-"Doin' It."
-Yeah, "Doin' It."

00:06:12.638 --> 00:06:14.340
That was like 'cause,
"Hey, we're doin' it."

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.175
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:17.176
Wow. Yeah.

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:18.844
Some of them
are real bad, too, yeah.

00:06:18.844 --> 00:06:22.314
"Vinyl Reality."
-I like "Vinyl Reality."

00:06:22.314 --> 00:06:23.983
-Yeah, that was before
"Virtual Reality," right?

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:25.418
-These are big swings
you're taking.

00:06:25.418 --> 00:06:27.919
-I'm taking -- Yeah,
I'm taking big swings on this.

00:06:27.919 --> 00:06:29.888
Oh, my gosh. "Gather No Moss"?

00:06:29.888 --> 00:06:32.024
That's a little embarrassing.

00:06:32.024 --> 00:06:33.459
But I thought "Rolling..."
Yeah, yeah.

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:35.060
-I like it.
-Yeah, nope.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:37.396
I'm so happy you
went with "Almost Famous."

00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:39.332
-It seems to fit.

00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:40.966
-You know, the last time --
I'm not kidding.

00:06:40.966 --> 00:06:44.002
The last time I grew a beard
in my entire life ever

00:06:44.002 --> 00:06:45.938
was for you and for this movie.

00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:48.908
This is me as my chara--
your character, Dennis Hope,

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:51.109
in "Almost Famous."

00:06:51.109 --> 00:06:52.611
Yeah, that's me
right there, everybody.

00:06:52.611 --> 00:06:54.714
Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:54.714 --> 00:06:56.048
Thank you.

00:06:58.383 --> 00:07:02.021
Is Dennis Hope -- is my
character still in the play?

00:07:02.021 --> 00:07:03.890
-I'm really glad
you bring this up,

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:06.758
because, Jimmy, we love
Dennis Hope of the movie,

00:07:06.758 --> 00:07:11.096
and, as you know, we became kind
of a family making the film

00:07:11.096 --> 00:07:12.431
because we were together
for a long time,

00:07:12.431 --> 00:07:14.166
kind of going through
rock school

00:07:14.166 --> 00:07:17.002
and teaching Billy Crudup how to
play guitar and everything.

00:07:17.002 --> 00:07:18.871
You came in and rocked
your scene so well

00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:22.074
as the manager of the future,
who kind of glimpses

00:07:22.074 --> 00:07:24.309
all that's coming in terms of
rock getting bigger.

00:07:24.309 --> 00:07:26.178
So of course it's in the play.

00:07:26.178 --> 00:07:30.649
And my dream has
always been to have you

00:07:30.649 --> 00:07:32.785
come and be the link between
the two casts,

00:07:32.785 --> 00:07:36.288
the cast of the musical,
who also became

00:07:36.288 --> 00:07:39.725
a really tight family,
and the movie.

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:42.561
So I would like you to come on
the Broadway stage

00:07:42.561 --> 00:07:44.997
and play your part again
of Dennis Hope.

00:07:44.997 --> 00:07:47.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:52.038
-Really?
-Yes.

00:07:55.074 --> 00:07:56.208
-I am in!

00:07:56.208 --> 00:07:59.345
I would be -- I would --
Absolutely, I am in.

00:07:59.345 --> 00:08:01.214
Oh, my gosh!
♪♪

00:08:04.416 --> 00:08:06.152
Who plays it now?

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:10.155
-We have a wonderful actor named
Jakeim Hart, a great musician.

00:08:10.155 --> 00:08:11.724
-I'll talk to Jakeim.
We'll work it out.

00:08:11.724 --> 00:08:13.292
-Jakeim will be around.

00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:16.562
But the thing is, like,
knowing you might step in,

00:08:16.562 --> 00:08:19.331
I rewrote the scene for,
you know,

00:08:19.331 --> 00:08:22.267
kind of advanced Jimmy fever.

00:08:22.267 --> 00:08:25.637
Like, I really wanted to get
a scene worthy of you, you know.

00:08:25.637 --> 00:08:28.540
And the beard.
-Yeah.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:31.077
-And so I wrote a new version
of the scene for you,

00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:33.845
which turned out to be so great
that that's what's in the play,

00:08:33.845 --> 00:08:35.514
and Jakeim does that version.

00:08:35.514 --> 00:08:36.782
-Is that right?
-Yeah.

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:39.652
So, it's got some new jokes
that I bring to you.

00:08:39.652 --> 00:08:40.819
And it's fun.

00:08:40.819 --> 00:08:42.454
But it's kind of like
a band, right?

00:08:42.454 --> 00:08:44.623
It's like the cast
is a little bit like a band.

00:08:44.623 --> 00:08:46.925
Come and jam with us.
-I want to jam.

00:08:46.925 --> 00:08:48.260
-Yeah.
-I'm going to jam with the band.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.563
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:51.563 --> 00:08:55.367
I also want to say,
in the playbill,

00:08:55.367 --> 00:08:57.169
you dedicate the show
to your mom,

00:08:57.169 --> 00:09:00.038
who, luckily, I got to meet.

00:09:00.038 --> 00:09:02.942
And she would have been
so proud of this.

00:09:02.942 --> 00:09:05.077
-Thanks, buddy.
-This is a giant deal, buddy.

00:09:05.077 --> 00:09:07.746
I'm so happy for you.
It is so fun.

00:09:07.746 --> 00:09:10.750
It is, again, musical.
It is funny.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:11.950
It's got heart.

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:14.353
And it's written by
an Oscar-winning writer.

00:09:14.353 --> 00:09:16.522
So what more do you want?

00:09:16.522 --> 00:09:17.756
I love you, buddy.
-Love you, man.

00:09:17.756 --> 00:09:20.893
-Cameron Crowe!
-Thank you.

00:09:20.893 --> 00:09:24.363
-When we come back,
the cast of the Broadway show

00:09:24.363 --> 00:09:27.466
"Almost Famous"
is performing "Tiny Dancer."

00:09:27.466 --> 00:09:30.002
Stick around, everybody.
Come on back!

